 1. a lambda names its parameter:                            map(lambda x: x * 2, xs) = [2, 4]
 2. a comprehension names it too:                            [x * 2 for x in xs] = [2, 4]
 3. two values are unpacked by name:                         [a + b for a, b in pairs] = [3, 7], enumerate: [0, 2]
 4. one parameter takes the whole pair, unpacking is spelled out: len(p) [2], p[0] + p[1] [3], a + b [3]
 5. signature and argcount:                                  lambda x: x (x) 1, lambda a, b: a (a, b) 2
 6. nested comprehensions name their own variable:           [[10, 20], [30]], no clash possible
 7. it and _1 are plain names, nothing to mix:               _1 = 5; it = 6 -> 5 6 (int)
 8. it beside an ordinary parameter is a parameter:          lambda x, it: it -> (x, it), called (1, 2) = 2
 9. a lambda mentioning it compiles; it is a free name:      co_names ('it',), called after del it: NameError
10. _ is an ordinary name in a script:                       _ = 7 -> 7; the REPL's last value and match's wildcard are separate conventions
